-- allium: 3
-- learnings.allium
--
-- Scope: the knowledge base. Agents record entries that land approved at once
--        and reach later agents through the dispatch prompt and
--        query_learnings. Curation is automatic: agents rate entries with
--        rate_learning, and a board sweep archives stale, unvalued ones. There
--        is no human approval step and no TUI surface.
-- Includes: the scope model, ranking, the MCP tools (record_learning,
--           query_learnings, rate_learning, delete_learning), dispatch-prompt
--           injection, the wrap-up rating nudge and the stale sweep.
-- Excludes: the entity and enum definitions (core.allium: Learning,
--           LearningScope, LearningKind, LearningStatus, Retrieval,
--           LearningVerdict, VerdictKind, RetrievalSource); who may see a
--           learning (workspaces.allium: LearningVisibility).

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./dispatch.allium" as dispatch
use "./epics.allium" as epics
use "./store-split.allium" as split

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Storage
------------------------------------------------------------

-- core/Learning, core/Retrieval and core/LearningVerdict are shared store
-- tables. A learning recorded on any host's board is visible, ranked and
-- rateable from every other board at once; what a reader sees is decided by
-- the learning's own scope, scope_ref and workspace (see Scope Model), not by
-- the machine it was recorded on.
--
-- The store keeps one verdict per (task, learning): a repeat from the same
-- task replaces the earlier one, so a task moves a learning's upvote_count by
-- at most one in either direction (RateLearningViaMcp). The verdict table is
-- part of the snapshot (spacetime-seed.allium: SharedTable).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Scope Model
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A learning's scope decides which dispatches receive it. A dispatch takes
-- the union of the learnings that match any of the task's anchors:
--
--   scope     scope_ref matches              included when
--   --------  -----------------------------  ------------------------------
--   user      null                           always
--   repo      task.repo_path, or a parent    always (every task has a
--             directory of it                repo_path)
--   epic      str(task.epic_id)              the task belongs to an epic
--
-- A repo scope_ref covers a repo_path that equals it or lies under it at a
-- path separator (a trailing "/" is ignored): "/work/terraform/gcp" covers
-- every repo below it, and not "/work/terraform/gcp-old" (core.allium:
-- Learning.applies_to_dispatch, repo_ref_covers). One entry can so serve a
-- family of repos that share a parent directory, without the user scope,
-- which reaches every repo.
--
-- There is no task scope. A learning scoped to one task would never reach a
-- dispatch prompt or a query_learnings answer, not even for its own task: a
-- re-dispatch or phoenix copy gets a new task id. record_learning refuses
-- scope=task and points the agent to repo or epic (RecordLearningViaMcp).
-- Rows recorded under the old scope were archived and re-scoped to repo once
-- (the store's one-time retire step, run on the first connection after the
-- module that dropped the scope is published).
-- A row whose task sat under a private epic (anywhere up the chain) loses its
-- workspace, so it stays with its author as it did before; one with no author
-- keeps its workspace, as none would show it to everyone.
--
-- When record_learning omits scope_ref, the scope supplies it:
--   scope=user    → null
--   scope=repo    → task.repo_path
--   scope=epic    → str(task.epic_id), refused when the task has no epic
--
-- Who sees a learning (workspaces.allium: LearningVisibility, D8). The store
-- stamps author and workspace on every new learning; a client cannot choose
-- them.
--   scope=user    → no workspace; only the author sees it.
--   scope=epic    → the epic's workspace.
--   scope=repo    → the workspace of the recording task's epic; none when the
--                   task is on the main board or there is no task.
-- A learning with no workspace is private to its author. A learning with a
-- workspace is seen by that workspace's members, and an epic-scoped
-- one only while its item is also visible to the reader. A learning with no
-- author and no workspace is visible to everyone.
--
-- Every read path applies this one predicate for the caller: query_learnings,
-- the dispatch-prompt injection, get_learning, rate_learning and
-- delete_learning. An unseen learning is never returned and never gets a
-- retrieval row, and acting on it answers "not found".

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Ranking
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Dispatch injection and query_learnings rank candidates the same way.
-- Only approved entries inside the scope union are candidates; an entry
-- outside the union is never returned, however similar it is.
--
--   1. The query text is embedded and compared by cosine similarity with
--      each candidate's stored embedding. A candidate below 0.25 is dropped.
--   2. Each survivor scores
--        cosine * (1 + scope_weight) + upvote_boost + tag_boost
--      where scope_weight is epic 0.30, repo 0.20, user 0.10;
--      upvote_boost is 0.005 per net upvote, counting 0 to 10; and
--      tag_boost is 0.05 per tag the entry shares with the call's tag_filter.
--   3. Results come highest score first.
--
-- tag_filter raises a score; it never excludes an entry.
--
-- These numbers, the dispatch injection cap of 5 and the query_learnings
-- limit cap of 50 are fixed by design, not settings: they tune one ranking
-- that both paths share, and a per-host value would make two boards inject
-- different knowledge for the same task.
--
-- kind = procedural does not change the rank or where the entry renders.
-- It changes only how the entry is written (RecordLearningViaMcp).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

-- An entry lands approved and is retrievable at once. The one status change
-- any rule makes is approved → archived (ArchiveStaleLearning), and archived
-- is terminal. rejected has no inbound edge; stored rows may still hold it,
-- and like archived ones they are excluded from retrieval. The transition
-- graph is core/Learning's.
--
-- There is no edit. summary, detail, kind and tags are set once by
-- record_learning; a wrong entry is deleted (delete_learning) and recorded
-- again, or left to be downvoted and swept. A verdict never changes status.
--
-- Every surfacing of a learning to a task writes a core/Retrieval row: source
-- prompt_injection for the dispatch prompt and query_learnings for that
-- tool. The enum's procedural value is never written. A repeat surfacing
-- writes another row.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

rule AugmentDispatchPromptWithLearnings {
    when: dispatch/AgentLaunched(task, mode)

    -- A resume continues the agent's earlier conversation and builds no new
    -- prompt, so it injects nothing.
    requires: mode in {standard, quick, research}

    let candidates = core/Learnings where applies_to_dispatch(task) and not off_topic_in_repo(task.repo_path)
    -- The at most 5 best-ranked candidates (see Ranking), ranked against the
    -- task's title and description.
    let relevant = top_ranked_for_dispatch(candidates, task)

    ensures:
        for l in relevant:
            core/Retrieval.created(task: task, learning: l, source: prompt_injection, retrieved_at: now)
        if relevant.count > 0:
            DispatchPromptAugmented(task: task, learnings: relevant)

    @guidance
        -- relevant renders as one "## Validated knowledge for this task"
        -- block in the dispatch prompt of every mode it fires for, procedural
        -- entries and others alike. With no relevant entry there is no block.
        -- Every entry in the block gets a prompt_injection retrieval, which
        -- is what lets the agent rate it.
        --
        -- Off-topic mute. candidates leaves out a learning that is
        -- off_topic_in_repo(task.repo_path): among the standing
        -- core/LearningVerdict rows on it from tasks with this task's
        -- repo_path, at least core/config.off_topic_verdicts_to_mute are
        -- not_applicable and none is helped. wrong verdicts count neither
        -- way; a deleted task's verdicts are gone with it.
        --   - Dispatch injection only. QueryLearningsViaMcp is unchanged, so
        --     an agent that asks can still find a muted entry.
        --   - Per repo. An entry muted in repo A still injects in repo B.
        --   - Before ranking and the cap. A muted entry's slot goes to the
        --     next-ranked candidate.
        --   - Reversible. One later helped verdict from a task in that repo
        --     un-mutes it, since the mute needs zero helped.
        --   - Not a score change. upvote_count, status and updated_at are
        --     untouched and the entry is not archived.
        --   - Derived from the verdict rows at each dispatch; nothing new is
        --     stored.
        -- Why: similarity alone injects many entries that do not fit, and
        -- not_applicable is spread thinly over many entries, so pruning
        -- cannot fix it. Muting per repo removes the repeat misses while an
        -- entry stays available everywhere it has fitted.
        --
        -- The block's own header carries the rating instruction: it names
        -- the full call ("rate_learning(learning_id, task_id, verdict) —
        -- helped if it applied, wrong if it misled you") and asks for
        -- not_applicable on an entry the agent read but did not use because
        -- it did not fit. The header renders only when entries were
        -- injected, which is exactly when there is something to rate, so the
        -- prompt's shared trailing line does not repeat it
        -- (dispatch-prompt.allium: ThePromptNamesNoToolMerelyToSayItExists).
}

rule WrapUpRateLearningNudge {
    when: split/WrapUpRequested(task, host, action)

    requires: action = pr
    requires: task.host = host

    ensures: RateLearningNudged(task: task)

    @guidance
        -- The wrap_up success response for the pr action asks the agent to
        -- rate (helped | wrong | not_applicable) any entry surfaced to it
        -- this session and not yet rated, and says only surfaced entries can
        -- be rated. rebase and done append nothing, and a request the module
        -- refuses (store-split.allium: RequestWrapUp) gets no nudge. It is a
        -- nudge, not a gate: exit_session needs no rating.
        -- No setting gates it, and it never mentions record_learning. The
        -- setting-gated record_learning nudge is a different behaviour on a
        -- different tool (mcp-task-tools.allium:
        -- PrFinalisationReflectionNudge), and the normal wrap_up →
        -- exit_session path never shows it, because that path closes the
        -- task through the module, not through update_tasks.
        -- Reflection proper is the /retro skill, which the /wrap-up skill
        -- runs before its commit step, for all three actions.
}

rule RecordLearningViaMcp {
    when: McpRecordLearning(task, kind, summary, scope, detail?, scope_ref?, tags?)

    requires: summary != ""
    requires: scope = user implies scope_ref = null
    requires: scope in {repo, epic} implies scope_ref != null
    requires: not references_internal_code(summary) and not references_internal_code(detail)
    requires: kind = procedural implies (detail != null and detail != "")

    ensures: core/Learning.created(
        kind: kind,
        summary: summary,
        detail: detail,
        scope: scope,
        scope_ref: scope_ref ?? default_scope_ref(task, scope),
        tags: tags ?? {},
        status: approved,
        source_task: task,
        upvote_count: 0,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- scope is one of user, repo, epic (core.allium: LearningScope).
        -- scope=task is refused: "scope=task is not supported; record the
        -- learning with scope=repo or scope=epic". The store's
        -- validate_learning_scope refuses it too, so no write path stores it.
        -- default_scope_ref is the table in Scope Model. An explicit repo
        -- scope_ref is stored with `~` expanded and no trailing "/". A blank
        -- or whitespace-only summary counts as empty.
        --
        -- The embedding is computed from summary, detail, kind and tags in
        -- the same write as the row, so a stored learning never carries an
        -- embedding of other values (EmbeddingConsistency). A learning with no
        -- embedding is invisible to both retrieval paths; a startup repair
        -- pass derives the missing ones from the stored fields, changes
        -- nothing else, and only logs a failure.
        --
        -- The reply says the entry is active and, for a scope other than
        -- task, that matching dispatches will receive it. It then lists up to
        -- 5 approved learnings of the same kind, scope
        -- and scope_ref, and warns against keeping a duplicate when one of
        -- them already says the same thing. It does not suggest rating them:
        -- they are not retrievals for this task, so rate_learning would
        -- refuse. Nothing else deduplicates.
        --
        -- references_internal_code(text) rejects summary or detail containing:
        -- allow-phantom-symbol: describes the citation shape itself, not a real reference
        --   - a `path.rs::symbol` citation (a Rust file path plus a `::`-qualified
        --     symbol), or
        --   - a `Type::method` citation (a PascalCase type plus a `::`-qualified
        --     method), or
        --   - a bare snake_case identifier with four or more underscores,
        --     backticked or not, or
        --   - a call with empty parentheses — an identifier immediately
        --     followed by "()", backticked or not, or
        --   - a macro invocation — an identifier followed by "!", where the
        --     identifier contains an underscore or the "!" is followed by an
        --     opening bracket, or
        --   - a source-file reference: a token carrying a file extension that
        --     either contains a directory separator, or whose extension names
        --     a programming language. A bare manifest or document filename
        --     with no directory separator is not one. A token reached through
        --     a URL scheme or carrying a "www." prefix is exempt; a bare
        --     hostname with a path is not.
        -- The rejection names the rule the text broke. The first three shapes
        -- mirror three of check-doc-symbols.sh's four candidate shapes. Unlike
        -- that script, this is an unconditional reject, not an existence
        -- check: a citation that resolves today rots as easily as one that
        -- never did, and nothing re-checks the knowledge base against the
        -- code. The script's fourth shape (a bare backticked snake_case span)
        -- is not rejected, because MCP tool names (query_learnings, wrap_up)
        -- have that shape and are a stable public interface.
        --
        -- WHERE THE CHECK STOPS. A bare PascalCase word and a short
        -- snake_case identifier are implementation detail too, but no regex
        -- separates them from a product, library or lint name, so the
        -- /learnings skill keeps them out, not the validator. There is no
        -- escape hatch: nobody reviews the knowledge base, so an override in
        -- agent-written text could not be enforced. Code citations belong in
        -- a spec or a doc comment, which are checked and have one.
        --
        -- A procedural learning must carry a non-blank detail, which names
        -- the boundary: where the agent stops following the entry and asks a
        -- human. Only the detail's presence is enforced; that it names a
        -- boundary is the /learnings skill's convention.
}

rule QueryLearningsViaMcp {
    when: McpQueryLearnings(task, query?, tag_filter?, limit?)

    -- The ranked entries the call returns: the scope union for task, ranked
    -- as Ranking says against query, or against the task's title and
    -- description when query is omitted; at most limit of them (default and
    -- cap 50; a limit below 1 counts as 1).
    let returned = ranked_learnings(task, query, tag_filter, limit)

    ensures:
        for l in returned:
            core/Retrieval.created(task: task, learning: l, source: query_learnings, retrieved_at: now)
        LearningsQueried(task: task, query: query, tag_filter: tag_filter, limit: limit)

    @guidance
        -- Unlike dispatch injection, the off-topic mute does not apply here,
        -- so an agent that asks can still find a muted entry. Every returned
        -- entry gets a query_learnings retrieval, which makes it rateable.
}

rule RateLearningViaMcp {
    when: McpRateLearning(learning, task, verdict)

    -- Checked before the retrieval: a learning the caller cannot see (never
    -- retrieved, or retrieved and since moved out of sight) is answered "not
    -- found", never "was not retrieved" and never "recorded".
    requires: learning.visible_to(caller())
    -- Only an entry surfaced to this task, by injection or query_learnings,
    -- can be rated. An unknown verdict string is refused too.
    requires: exists core/Retrieval{task: task, learning: learning}

    -- The verdict this task already gave this learning, if any.
    let standing = core/LearningVerdict{task, learning}
    let previous = if standing != null: standing.verdict else: null

    ensures:
        -- The standing verdict is now `verdict`; the score moves by the
        -- difference between the two (helped = +1, wrong = -1,
        -- not_applicable = 0, none = 0).
        if standing = null:
            core/LearningVerdict.created(task: task, learning: learning, verdict: verdict)
        else:
            standing.verdict = verdict
        learning.upvote_count = learning.upvote_count + weight(verdict) - weight(previous)
        if verdict = helped and previous != helped:
            learning.last_upvoted_at = now
        if weight(verdict) != weight(previous):
            learning.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- helped means the entry applied, wrong that it misled (the score
        -- may go negative), not_applicable that the agent read it and it did
        -- not fit. not_applicable weighs 0: on a first verdict the learning
        -- row is not written at all, so it cannot keep a stale entry alive.
        -- It feeds the per-repo dispatch mute
        -- (AugmentDispatchPromptWithLearnings) instead.
        -- One verdict stands per (task, learning), so the prompt's "rate when
        -- you act" and the wrap-up rating step cannot count one use twice.
        -- The same verdict again is a no-op; helped then wrong moves the
        -- score by -2. last_upvoted_at is not rolled back when a helped is
        -- replaced.
        -- The retrieval check is the store's (the apply_learning_verdicts
        -- reducer), so every client gets the same answer.
}

rule DeleteLearningViaMcp {
    when: McpDeleteLearning(learning_id)

    let learning = core/Learning{id: learning_id}

    requires: exists learning

    ensures: not exists learning

    @guidance
        -- Permanent. An id that names no learning, or one the caller cannot
        -- see, answers not found (-32001), not invalid params (-32602).
}

rule ReScopeLearningsOnRepoGroupDelete {
    when: epics/DeleteEpic(sub)

    requires: sub.origin = repo_group
    requires: sub.parent != null

    ensures:
        for l in core/Learnings where l.scope = epic and l.scope_ref = str(sub.id):
            l.scope_ref = str(sub.parent.id)
            l.workspace = sub.parent.workspace

    @guidance
        -- A repo-group sub-epic is deleted when grouping is turned off
        -- (FlattenEpic, or a feed epic's FlatFeedReconcile) or when a task's
        -- repo_path change leaves it empty. Its epic-scoped learnings move to
        -- the parent so they are not orphaned, in the same store transaction
        -- as the delete, so the two land together or not at all. scope_ref
        -- is not an embedding input, so nothing is re-embedded. The workspace
        -- follows the target epic; a sub-epic shares its parent's, so on this
        -- path it does not change.
        --
        -- A hard delete of a grouped root epic deletes its sub-epics with it
        -- and re-scopes nothing, like any other epic delete.
}

rule ArchiveStaleLearning {
    when: learning: core/Learning.updated_at + core/config.stale_learning_threshold <= now

    requires: core/config.stale_learning_cleanup_enabled = true
    requires: learning.status = approved
    requires: learning.upvote_count <= 0
    -- The board's sweep archives only what this board's person sees
    -- (workspaces.allium: LearningVisibility, WritesRequireSight). The store
    -- operator sees every learning.
    requires: learning.visible_to(core/local_host().owner) or is_operator(core/local_host().owner)

    ensures:
        learning.status = archived
        learning.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Keeps unvalued entries out of the retrieval pool with no human in
        -- the loop. An entry that earned even one net upvote is never swept,
        -- however old; only delete_learning, or enough wrong verdicts to
        -- bring its score back to zero or below, removes it.
        -- Each board checks from its tick (core/Tick), at most once an hour,
        -- and archives every eligible entry it sees in one store call. An
        -- entry stays eligible while it stays untouched, so the cadence only
        -- delays the archive.
        -- Archiving sets updated_at and leaves the entry not approved, so it
        -- fires once.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- EmbeddingConsistency. A learning's stored embedding is always the one its
-- summary, detail, kind and tags produce: the two are written together
-- (RecordLearningViaMcp). It is a property of each write, not of one state,
-- so it is stated here in prose.
--
-- No two approved learnings should share (kind, summary, scope, scope_ref).
-- Nothing enforces it; record_learning's reply shows similar entries so the
-- recording agent can avoid or delete a duplicate (RecordLearningViaMcp).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The knowledge base's only interactive boundary: the four MCP tools.
-- Nothing in the TUI reads or writes learnings.
surface LearningMcpTools {
    facing _: core/McpClient

    provides:
        McpRecordLearning(task, kind, summary, scope, detail?, scope_ref?, tags?)
        McpQueryLearnings(task, query?, tag_filter?, limit?)
        McpRateLearning(learning, task, verdict)
        McpDeleteLearning(learning_id)

    @guarantee TheAuthoringRulesHaveOneHome
        -- The record_learning tool description is one sentence: it records a
        -- non-obvious finding to the knowledge base, and points at the
        -- /learnings skill for how to write one. Every authoring rule — name
        -- no code, a procedural entry states its boundary, the triage
        -- question, the record/don't-record lists, the kind and scope tables,
        -- how to write a summary — lives in the skill alone. A second copy in
        -- the description would reach every agent on every tools/list and
        -- drift from the skill. The two rules the validator enforces still
        -- reach a caller without the skill when they matter: the refusal
        -- names the rule the text broke (mcp-task-tools.allium:
        -- ToolDescriptionsStateBehaviourAndNameNoCode).
        --
        -- The skill also carries the triage an agent applies before writing
        -- prose about code: could a failing check for a violation be written
        -- from the source alone? If yes, it is a lint rule: write the check
        -- and record nothing. If no, it is judgment, and prose is right.
}
